Step 0 — Connect to your GCP Ubuntu server

You already used PuTTY previously.

Connect using:

Host Name: YOUR_GCP_EXTERNAL_IP
Port: 22
Connection: SSH

Login with your Ubuntu username.

Then verify:

whoami

You should see something like:

sunil

Check OS:

cat /etc/os-release

You should see Ubuntu information.

Check server:

hostname

And:

uptime
Step 1 — Update Ubuntu

Run:

sudo apt update

Then:

sudo apt upgrade -y

This may take a few minutes.

Verify:

sudo apt update

You should see that package information is up to date.

Step 2 — Check whether Docker is already installed

Run:

docker --version

If you get:

Command 'docker' not found

that's okay.

Also check:

docker ps

If Docker isn't installed, we'll install it.

Step 3 — Install Docker

For this lab, install Docker using Ubuntu's Docker package:

sudo apt install docker.io -y

After installation:

docker --version

You should get something similar to:

Docker version 28.x.x

The exact version can differ.

Step 4 — Start Docker

Run:

sudo systemctl start docker

Enable Docker at boot:

sudo systemctl enable docker

Check:

sudo systemctl status docker

You want:

Active: active (running)

Press:

q

to exit the status screen.

Step 5 — Test Docker

