🎉 Your Docker engine is working.

Step 6 — Avoid typing sudo every time

Currently you may need:

sudo docker ps

We can allow your Ubuntu user to use Docker.

Run:

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

Then:

newgrp docker

Now test:

docker ps

If it works without sudo, you're ready.

Verify:

docker info
Step 7 — Download Nginx

Now we enter the actual project.

Run:

docker pull nginx:alpine

You'll see Docker downloading the Nginx image.

Why nginx:alpine?

nginx
  +
Alpine Linux
  =
small Docker image

This is useful for production because smaller images generally mean less storage and faster image transfers.

Check the image:

docker images

You should see something similar to:

REPOSITORY   TAG      IMAGE ID      CREATED       SIZE
nginx        alpine   xxxxxxxx      ...           ...
Step 8 — Run Nginx container

Now run:

docker run -d --name production-web -p 8080:80 nginx:alpine

Understand this carefully:

docker run
   |
   +-- -d
   |     Background mode
   |
   +-- --name production-web
   |     Container name
   |
   +-- -p 8080:80
   |     Host port : Container port
   |
   +-- nginx:alpine
         Docker image

The important part is:

-p 8080:80

Meaning:

GCP Server
Port 8080
     |
     v
Docker Container
Port 80
     |
     v
Nginx
